WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
BUMILLER: Welcome, everyone.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Thank you very much.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Thank you, everybody.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.000
My name's Elisabeth Bumiller.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.330
I'm the Washington Bureau chief of The New
York Times here.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:11.630
And welcome to the Council on Foreign Relations
keynote panel discussion to conclude the symposium,

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.929
"The New Geopolitics of China, India, and
Pakistan."

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:16.410
This is a very good panel.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.430
We've got three ambassadors up here.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:26.880
To my immediate right is Ambassador J. Stapleton
Roy, who's a former U.S. ambassador to China.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.460
Long list of-you have his biography.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.470
I imagine many of you already know him.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.390
So here he is, the legend.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.390
(Laughter.)

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.640
Next to him is somebody-
ROY: I'm not that old.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.640
(Laughter.)

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.180
BUMILLER: I know you also know Ambassador
Wisner-Frank G. Wisner, former U.S. ambassador

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.350
to India, many other things.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.950
And then finally, Ambassador Daniel Feldman,
former U.S. special representative for the-for

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:50.950
Afghanistan and Pakistan.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:57.710
He was deeply involved as a senior official
in setting up that office under Richard Holbrooke,

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:04.580
and worked in that office in some very tense
years, from 2009 to 2015.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.060
So what I'd like to do today, this is on the
record.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.479
I'm going to engage our ambassadors for half
an hour myself.

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:18.190
And then we're going to open up to questions
from the audience for the second half hour.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.939
I'd like to start with Ambassador Feldman.

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:26.070
Just talk-I'd like you to talk a little bit
about your experiences with Pakistan during

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:32.520
a very volatile period in U.S.-Pakistani relations,
specifically after the 2011 raid on Abbottabad

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.430
that killed Osama bin Laden, and how you helped
manage the relationship with Pakistan after

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.510
that.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:41.579
And personal recollections would be most welcome.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:50.829
And then just talk a bit about the nosedive
the relationship took, how you helped to bring

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:52.900
it back, and a bit of where we are now.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.590
I'll ask the others to also recount their
experiences in their specific countries.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:03.610
And then we're going to have everyone pull
together what this means for the region and

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.190
the geopolitics at the end.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.250
So, Dan, take it away.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.489
FELDMAN: Well, thanks.

00:02:08.489 --> 00:02:10.129
And very good to be here.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:13.790
On that question in particular I should call
Marc Grossman back up to the stage, since

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.040
he was the special representative at the time,
and on the ground working on many of these

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.959
issues.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.450
And I was just supporting him from Washington.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.700
But I think certainly that period in 2011
was one of enormous shocks to the relationship.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:33.950
I served in the special representative office
and then as special representative for the

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.030
final year, but over the six and a half years,
from the very beginning of the administration

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.650
in 2009, until last fall.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.379
And I divide my time into kind of three discrete
periods.

00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:52.349
And with the asterisks that we're, you know,
potentially on the cusp of a fourth, but I

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:54.020
hope that we're not.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.750
The first one was in that period, 2009 to
2010, when we were really reassessing our

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:03.160
relationship with Pakistan, and sought to
elevate it to these new heights.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.409
We elevated our strategic dialogue to the
ministerial level.

00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:12.080
We had several visits by Secretary Clinton
to Pakistan, and visits here by Pakistani

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:13.080
senior officials.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.740
We passed Kerry-Lugar-Berman and the promise
of $7 1/2 billion in civilian assistance over

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.040
the course of five years and beyond.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.500
We were reengaging in Afghanistan with the
troop and civilian surge.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.799
And therefore, Pakistan was critical for that
piece as well.

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:37.519
And so it had such high expectations that
it almost certainly wasn't sustainable, and

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:38.519
then it wasn't.

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:41.720
So in 2011, obviously, we saw these series
of shocks.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.780
Abbottabad was actually just one of them.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.659
It started with Ray Davis at the beginning
of 2011.

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:49.260
Followed by Abbottabad-
BUMILLER: Remind the audience.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.680
I mean, most of you know, but just remind
the audience.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.920
FELDMAN: Ray Davis, the U.S. contractor based
at our consulate who was involved in an incident

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.799
in killing several Pakistanis, and then it
turned into a significant issue.

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:07.159
I actually in the course of that-it was right
when Marc was starting his tenure as special

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:08.159
representative.

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:12.379
And I think the day he started I was on a
plane with Senator Kerry as we went there

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:16.420
to try to see what was possible, and meeting
with Pakistani civilian and military leadership.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.950
He was ultimately released.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:25.110
We were starting to just get back on track
and in May we had the Abbottabad raid, which-I

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:30.150
know Marc was on the ground in Pakistan soon
after that, and then Secretary Clinton went

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.590
with Admiral Mullen.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.639
By the end of May, after some conversations,
she went again.

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:46.220
And in October, with-what was it-I think it
was General Petraeus and General Dempsey to

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:52.530
have as coordinated a civilian-military intelligence
discussion with senior leadership as possible.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:57.340
Again, we were kind of just working through
that, and then we had the Salala incident

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:04.060
just before Thanksgiving in 2011, a cross-border
incident where U.S. jets inadvertently killed

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.650
24 Pakistani military members.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:12.570
And it really sent the relationship into the
deep freeze, to the point where they closed

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:18.070
the ground lines of communication supplying
our troops in Afghanistan.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:23.600
And we really worked very, very assiduously,
under Marc's leadership, over the course of

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.750
the first part of 2012 to finally put the
relationship onto firmer footing.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:34.590
And we achieved that in the summer of 2012,
interestingly, in a State Department diplomatic

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:39.700
channel between our deputy secretary at the
time, Tom Nides, and the finance minister,

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:46.370
Shaikh, in Pakistan, but which was the best
conduit for conversations.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:55.569
And since 2012, we've really been executing
a much more realistic, much more clear-eyed,

00:05:55.569 --> 00:06:02.770
more focused effort at engagement, which really
revolves around building on our core areas

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:10.289
of joint interest on counterterrorism, on
reconciliation, on nuclear nonproliferation,

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:13.169
on economic sustainability and energy.

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:18.830
And so we scaled back the strategic dialogue,
but kept it quite focused.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:24.450
And this was really the mechanism which kept
our relationship alive during the kind of

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.639
annus horribilis.

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:33.830
And over the last three and a half to four
years, it's really borne fruit.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:41.190
And I think that we have to all be mindful
that despite the continuing challenges-and

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:45.910
there are no doubt that there are very, very
significant challenges still to the relationship

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:52.590
and particularly around counterterrorism issues
with-and proxy groups, whether it's Haqqani,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.160
or LeT, or others.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.680
But there have been real significant developments
in Pakistan over the course of the last three

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:01.729
and a half to four years.

00:07:01.729 --> 00:07:09.080
The first democratic transition of a civilian
government, a gradual strengthening of civilian

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:13.540
leadership and that calibration between the
civilian and military leadership.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:19.080
Certainly increased strides in security, whether
that's in Karachi or the North Waziristan

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.130
operation.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:27.220
The engagement of Pakistan in much more regional
efforts, including the quite significant outreach

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:33.240
to Afghanistan, the effort to try to bring
the Taliban to the table, the quadrilateral

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.910
engagement with China and others.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:41.840
Much better economic sustainability than we'd
seen in decades, in terms of adherence to

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:48.401
the IMF program, grappling with their own
extremist issues, whether as a result of the

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:57.180
Peshawar school massacre, executing Qadri,
the person who murdered Governor Taseer.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.169
But moving forward incrementally.

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:06.820
And I worry that in the last few months, and
especially as some of these flash points have

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:17.190
come to light with the F-16 sale, with coalition
support funds, certainly the limitations of

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:24.610
Afghan patience and tolerance for what Pakistan
can do, and on what time table, as evidence

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:29.259
by President Ghani's comments after the most
recent, you know, horrific attack in Kabul

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:37.690
a few weeks ago, that we're going to forget
the lessons we learned in 2011 and '12, and

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:41.270
the importance of continued engagement.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:46.330
I want to make sure that we are able to exercise
every bit of leverage that we can.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:50.630
And there's been a lot of discussion in this
earlier this morning about what the limits

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:54.339
of U.S. power are, and how we utilize it.

00:08:54.339 --> 00:09:02.420
But I do want to make sure that we are able
to have at our-that we're able to access every

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:06.880
bit of that, whether that's security assistance,
or civilian assistance, or diplomatic engagement,

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:12.220
or intel cooperation, or incentivizing regional
structures, because at the end of the day

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:17.450
I firmly believe that it's in our national
security interest for us to continue to have

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.770
this realistic but very engaged relationship
with Pakistan.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:27.750
It's important because, for the first time
in six and a half years, we don't have a time-based

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.670
condition on our troops in Afghanistan.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.880
So it's having 9,800 troops there currently.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.820
Which we'll have to see, come the next administration,
how long that's continued.

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:41.940
But certainly whether it's just the logistics
of continuing to help supply them or, much

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:47.200
more importantly in my mind, the engagement
of Pakistan in any sort of long-term political

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:52.310
resolution, that would have to come about
for Afghanistan's stability and the reconciliation

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:53.540
process.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:58.050
We'll want to engage Pakistan, whether it's
nuclear proliferation, whether it's continued

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:02.500
extremism from the second-most populous Muslim
nation in the world.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:07.180
For all these reasons, we have to continue
on the path that we started down three and

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:08.890
a half years ago.

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:11.220
And it won't be easy.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:12.630
And it won't be short term.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:14.230
But it's important and in our interests.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.610
BUMILLER: Thank you.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:21.000
Frank, do you want to talk a bit about your
experience as the ambassador in Delhi from

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.000
'94 to '97.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:31.690
And that was just the beginnings of India's,
you know, economic growth, just the stirrings

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:32.690
of it.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:37.279
And you were also helping to manage relationship
between India and Pakistan.

00:10:37.279 --> 00:10:40.270
I imagine China was not as big a concern then,
but it was growing.

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:41.270
WISNER: It was.

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:42.270
BUMILLER: OK.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.380
So tell us about that, your life at Roosevelt
House.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.810
WISNER: Elisabeth, thank you.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:54.730
Perhaps rather than sort of go into excessive
detail about events that took place 22 years

00:10:54.730 --> 00:11:02.339
ago, maybe I could take a step back and draw
about three broad conclusions about where

00:11:02.339 --> 00:11:10.580
we and the Indians have been over these 22
years, starting where I was in the mid part

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:11.580
of the 1990s.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:12.580
BUMILLER: OK.

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:18.920
WISNER: And I stood then, and I took off to
go to India, in fact, attended a lunch before

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:24.390
I was even publicly an ambassador with President
Clinton at the time, and P.V.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:25.390
Narasimha Rao.

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:32.500
The only subject of strategic significance
we discussed was the dispute over almond trade.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.510
Now, why that?

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:41.990
Because in effect, our relationship was really
frozen over the issue of proliferation.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.880
Was India going to test or not test?

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:50.450
And an array of disincentives in the relationship
had grown up.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:57.769
And we and the Indians really did not have
a serious strategic vision of one another.

00:11:57.769 --> 00:12:03.389
If that was the starting point, gosh, what
22 years can do.

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:13.779
The main conclusion I draw, number one, is
that we now deeply believe that a strong India

00:12:13.779 --> 00:12:16.430
is good for the United States.

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:22.480
And that we have invested over the course
of two administrations-this one and the Bush

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:28.769
administration, just beginning at the very
end of the Clinton period-in measures to strengthen

00:12:28.769 --> 00:12:35.620
India economically, politically, and in defense
terms.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:43.230
And those have accelerated, particularly after
we got the nuclear problem out of the way,

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:50.339
in 2005 with the decision to settle a civil
nuclear agreement with India.

00:12:50.339 --> 00:12:58.760
Now, the interesting counterpart, of course,
has been that India herself has decided, while

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:06.760
maintaining her own stubbornly defended sense
of independence, that the United States is,

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:10.829
in India's mind today, a critical strategic
partner of India's.

00:13:10.829 --> 00:13:12.960
BUMILLER: Which did not exist back in the
days-

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.440
WISNER: It certainly didn't exist when I started.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:21.450
And if you want to just take a look at it
and think about it, in just a couple of weeks,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:26.880
June 7th and 8th, Prime Minister Modi will
be coming here and will be addressing a joint

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.060
session of Congress.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.130
Vajpayee did the same.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:37.720
Inconceivable in previous years in this relationship,
such has been the transformation.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:44.529
But having said that, being point number one,
point number two is be very careful as you

00:13:44.529 --> 00:13:47.430
look forward to think about this relationship.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:56.339
For India will be not a full-scale great power
anytime in the future.

00:13:56.339 --> 00:14:03.949
By 2050, if my memory is correct, India will
control about 7 percent of the world GDP.

00:14:03.949 --> 00:14:05.959
China will be controlling 20.

00:14:05.959 --> 00:14:09.420
We and the Europeans about 17 percent.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:16.880
Result is that India will be a lesser figure,
albeit an extremely important one.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:23.230
And India will be humbled furthermore by a
relatively weak economy that proves very difficult

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:32.500
to change and has major shortfalls in infrastructure,
and health, and education, tax systems.

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:38.110
But the second great weakness of the Indian
establishment is the weakness of Indian state

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:39.930
institutions.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.930
India has a brilliant diplomatic service.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:44.730
You could put it on the head of a pin.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:52.149
It has a tiny intelligence service compared
to the requirements of a major regional or

00:14:52.149 --> 00:14:54.050
even global power.

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:57.840
India's military is just now reforming itself.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:07.110
India has a way to go to grow into-to have
the armor of a significant power and player.

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:14.310
The third feature that humbles India is ironically
the other side of the equation that we like

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:15.310
about India.

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:20.070
It's a democracy, but it makes it very difficult
to get decisions made.

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:27.699
You have hotly contested politics in Delhi,
and then you have a diffusion of power throughout

00:15:27.699 --> 00:15:28.699
the states.

00:15:28.699 --> 00:15:34.149
So India doesn't move quickly to make decisions.

00:15:34.149 --> 00:15:37.639
She heads in a more glacial place.

00:15:37.639 --> 00:15:39.680
And I think that we have to get used to.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:48.620
But none of these points should take away
from our mind that India is a hugely important

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:56.110
player for us, especially in the Asia-Pacific
area in maintaining the balance of power.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:02.000
And so, Elisabeth, let me end with a third
point very quickly, is now to think forward.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:09.019
What should we be trying to accomplish as
Modi comes now and as a new administration

00:16:09.019 --> 00:16:10.019
follows on?

00:16:10.019 --> 00:16:18.329
And I think it's really important that we
stay focused on India reality and potential,

00:16:18.329 --> 00:16:20.610
recognizing the limitations.

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:25.699
But the way to do it is to stay focused on
what we're doing right now, which is promoting

00:16:25.699 --> 00:16:32.829
economic change and reform, promoting the
building of economic ties between our sector,

00:16:32.829 --> 00:16:39.699
private sector, and the Indian economy, and
moving robustly, as we have in defense cooperation,

00:16:39.699 --> 00:16:47.160
both in supply of defense articles, mil-mil
cooperation, intelligence sharing, these transformative

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:51.149
features that have made the relationship very
important.

00:16:51.149 --> 00:16:57.740
The second point that I think is just critical
is not only continuing what we're doing, but

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:02.199
deepening it, deepening it political.

00:17:02.199 --> 00:17:08.339
Thinking with the Indians about how we deal
with major questions like China, how we deal

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:14.949
with major regional questions in their near
neighborhood-Iran and the Middle East-and

00:17:14.949 --> 00:17:22.179
how we are prepared-what contingencies the
United States is prepared to act on, and how

00:17:22.179 --> 00:17:30.679
India would pay in those contingencies to
begin to structure a more sophisticated approach

00:17:30.679 --> 00:17:36.640
to India as a regional-cum-global partner
of the United States.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:42.590
And finally, in that regard, to steal a line
from Alyssa this morning, and that is we should

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:47.630
play for India's introduction to the global
high table.

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:53.660
We were right to put forward-or back the candidacy
of India for the Security Council.

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:57.520
I don't think it's going to happen any time
soon, but it's the right direction.

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:02.600
But APEC is a very smart move of the United
States.

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:10.679
Not that we can rule the roost of 40 countries,
but we certainly can help India get there.

00:18:10.679 --> 00:18:17.010
And India can help herself, to answer Evan's
reservations this morning, by looking at what

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:20.799
APEC does, the conventions, APEC's reach.

00:18:20.799 --> 00:18:26.440
Many of them match reforms India's already
undertaken, and show that she'd be interested

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:33.260
in playing in those fields as she becomes
a candidate for membership in APEC, and then

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:37.730
in a broader trade and political dimension
that would flow from it.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.260
So a bit of the past and a bit of the future.

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:41.260
BUMILLER: Thanks.

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:45.990
And now, Ambassador Roy, who overlapped a
bit when you were ambassador to China.

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:51.030
The same as when Frank was ambassador to India,
you were there, ambassador from '91 to '95.

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:54.789
I want you to-because we're-I want you to
sum up a bit.

00:18:54.789 --> 00:18:58.870
I mean, just before we open up for questions.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:02.690
I want you to talk about China and your role
there, but I also want you to answer the bigger,

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:08.640
larger question-because this is about the
geopolitics of China, India, and Pakistan-is

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:14.190
I think there was an earlier comment today
that the U.S. no longer has any ability to

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:18.630
shape outcomes in South Asia, Central Asia.

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:27.950
Even though we have spent 15 years in Afghanistan,
billions of dollars, we still have 9,800 troops

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:34.809
in Afghanistan indefinitely at this point,
Americans are in combat still in Afghanistan

00:19:34.809 --> 00:19:42.490
as we learned from the Kunduz bombing earlier
last year, but we have not invested economically

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:43.900
the way China has.

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:49.021
So I'd like you to take that on and talk about
China, but also answer that larger question

00:19:49.021 --> 00:19:55.720
of whether we can affect outcomes there, and
if we should be investing more economically,

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:56.720
competing with China.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:58.950
What do you think?

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:05.500
ROY: Let me begin with a little bit of history,
just to illustrate why I have found this program

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:09.250
today so fascinating.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:17.490
In anticipation of this opportunity today,
in 1945, after seven years in wartime China,

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:23.090
the U.S. Army Air Corps, which operated the
only airline at the time between China and

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:31.540
India, flew my family and me to Calcutta,
where I spent a week investigating the situation

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:33.000
on the ground there.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:40.930
We then took a train across India to Bombay,
where we spent a month.

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:44.520
The war in the Pacific was still continuing,
but we were able to get a ship back to New

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.610
York.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:56.230
Jump from there to 1962, when I was serving
in our embassy in China, which was located

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:59.880
in Taipei, Taiwan, of course.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:06.320
And I'd just gotten then when the war between
China and India broke out.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:11.490
And it's no secret that the sympathies of
the Americas were with the democratic Indians

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:15.010
in their struggle against the godless communists.

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:21.140
And '62 was not the high point of cross-strait
relations between Taiwan and China at the

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:22.660
time.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:27.100
And I discovered, to my interest, that the
sympathies of the government in Taiwan were

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:29.650
entirely with Beijing.

00:21:29.650 --> 00:21:36.460
They thought that Beijing was acting in defense
of China's interests.

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:40.789
So free China was on communist China's side
in the-in the '62 war.

00:21:40.789 --> 00:21:45.700
The United States was not on that side.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:51.230
It gave me a useful insight into the fact
that nationalism, in many ways, is more important

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:55.940
than ideology in determining in how you locate
your thing.

00:21:55.940 --> 00:22:04.850
Since then, while I was ambassador in Beijing
in 1992, I spent 10 days in Xinjiang and visited

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:12.840
Ili and Kashgar, which are the opening points
to Kyrgyzstan and to Tajikistan.

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:15.950
And of course, Central Asia had just become
independent.

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:22.590
They'd just opened the rail link through Ili
into Kyrgyzstan.

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:30.510
But it was after centuries of essentially
a closed situation because the Russian Empire

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:36.020
in Central Asia was established under Catherine
the Great, largely.

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:41.010
And at roughly the same time the Qing dynasty,
in China, in its early days, established Chinese

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:44.010
control over Xinjiang again.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:50.919
It had control there 1,500 years earlier.

00:22:50.919 --> 00:22:54.929
But during the Ming dynasty they were weak
in that area.

00:22:54.929 --> 00:23:01.330
And it was in that period when they established
Xinjiang as part of China again.

00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:09.250
I mention this because the whole purpose of
this session is to talk about the changing

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:13.750
geopolitics of this region.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:15.620
And I throw in Central Asia.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:18.200
And I was struck by the discussion this morning.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.630
Russia was barely mentioned.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:25.340
And I think it's an egregious error that we
are making to leave.

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:30.809
On East Asia I can spend, you know, a week-long
conference and Russia is barely touched on.

00:23:30.809 --> 00:23:37.590
And here we are, talking about South Asia,
and failing to mention that Russia has very

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:45.870
strong interests in the areas abutting on
Afghanistan, Pakistan, and India to the north.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:48.530
India, Pakistan are very conscious of this.

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:52.080
We, Americas, somehow seem to drop this out.

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:59.549
Now, this is relevant because we have three
things that have really happened that are

00:23:59.549 --> 00:24:02.900
affecting this dynamic in a big way.

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:05.620
Number one is the rise of China and India.

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:12.779
Thirty years ago they were big countries,
but they were not, shall we say, great powers.

00:24:12.779 --> 00:24:16.470
Now they have emerged these great powers,
and largely through economic development,

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:20.210
rather than their military strength.

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:25.950
The second is the opening up of Central Asia
with the collapse of the Soviet Empire in

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:34.429
Central Asia, which has created a no-man's
land for strategic competition among countries

00:24:34.429 --> 00:24:36.490
that are interested in the region.

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:40.720
And that includes Russia, it includes China,
it includes India.

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:46.520
And Pakistan, of course, is deeply concerned
about what happens in that part of the world.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:52.040
So enormous changes are taking effect.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:59.169
This is a transformational period, because
for the first time in recent history one great

00:24:59.169 --> 00:25:07.230
power-China-has the economic resources to
see the potential for extending its influence

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:15.320
into Central Asia and South Asia and the Middle
East through the development of communications,

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:18.809
transportation, trade.

00:25:18.809 --> 00:25:21.919
China is India's number-one trading partner.

00:25:21.919 --> 00:25:25.170
China is the number-one trading partner of
the Central Asian countries.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:28.870
China has this historic relationship with
Pakistan.

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:34.500
And our role in the region is a military role.

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:39.110
Back in the old days, we had an economic arm
to play.

00:25:39.110 --> 00:25:43.919
Now, essentially, we have a military hand
to play, and we either play it well or we

00:25:43.919 --> 00:25:46.230
play it badly.

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:52.679
But what's driving the transformational changes
in this region is economics.

00:25:52.679 --> 00:25:59.679
There is clearly a very important strategic
element to China's One Belt, One Road initiative,

00:25:59.679 --> 00:26:04.240
because China is-has an ambition to become
a global naval power.

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:09.190
It's already a global maritime power through
its civil maritime fleet.

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:15.279
But it's the economic element of China's move
into the region that is the transformational

00:26:15.279 --> 00:26:16.480
element.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:21.400
We've talked about Silk Road initiatives and
had no economic resources to back it up.

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:25.360
China has major economic resources to back
it up.

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:31.980
So, from the U.S. standpoint, our problem
is, essentially because of budgetary problems

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:37.720
in the United States, we're only funding our
military to be able to play a robust international

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:38.820
role.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:44.830
And if it turns out that the military component
of our national strength is inadequate to

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:51.480
produce the political outcomes that we would
like to see, and we're competing with countries

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:58.770
such as China who have major economic resources
to put behind it-even India's investments

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:03.120
in this area, I think, dwarf what we are prepared
to do at this present time.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:06.750
BUMILLER: Well, so here's my question, before
I open it up.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:10.330
What should-what should the United States
do in this case?

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:11.691
What is the role of the United States?

00:27:11.691 --> 00:27:16.110
Should the United States resist this in some
way, and try to spend billions of dollars

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:17.110
in the region?

00:27:17.110 --> 00:27:23.490
Or should the United States deal with what
seems to be reality and work in some way with-try

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:29.059
to work with China in the region and accept
that it's a new power in the region?

00:27:29.059 --> 00:27:33.429
ROY: I'll offer a quick view on this, because
I think my colleagues here will have-

00:27:33.429 --> 00:27:34.429
BUMILLER: Right.

00:27:34.429 --> 00:27:39.740
ROY: First of all, essentially, we have two
choices.

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:44.680
We're going to continue a more limited military
role in the area, so it's not that we're not

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:46.330
a player.

00:27:46.330 --> 00:27:52.020
But in terms of the economic drive, the infrastructure
development that is a very important transformational

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:58.200
element, we have a choice of either remaining
on the outside, playing a carping role, and

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:05.500
perhaps objecting to or trying to obstruct
the efforts by the other major players to

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:12.590
carve out their own role in the process; or
we can get involved in it as a junior partner.

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:16.149
We can't play the role of a major partner
because we don't have the economic resources

00:28:16.149 --> 00:28:17.539
to deal with it.

00:28:17.539 --> 00:28:26.260
But if we don't stay involved, we will not
be able to track what is going on effectively.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:32.420
The problem is, if we try to resist it, we're
going to show our weakening position in the

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:33.690
world.

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:37.299
I think that's the lesson we ought to learn
from AIIB.

00:28:37.299 --> 00:28:44.200
What happened is we saw that our closest European
allies were unprepared to take guidance from

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:47.630
the United States on whether or not they should
associate themselves with the bank.

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:54.140
We are going to show ourselves as a declining
power if we try to oppose forces that are

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:58.179
stronger than our resistance can possibly
be.

00:28:58.179 --> 00:29:04.840
So my judgment is we need to pay attention
to this world because the geopolitical transformations

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:11.470
taking place in the heart of the Eurasian
continent have global implications-involve

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:16.610
China, Russia, India, Pakistan, Japan, they're
all part of this game.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:21.270
And we need to be deeply involved in this,
and at the moment we only have the military

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:23.820
component to our strategy.

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:25.840
And I think that's a real limitation on what
we can do.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:29.430
BUMILLER: So are the other panelists in violent
agreement or disagreement?

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:30.430
(Laughs.)

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:37.030
WISNER: I can't resist wrestling with Strobe
because-excuse me, Stape-(laughter)-because

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:43.630
I really genuinely see the matters through
a different perspective.

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:52.190
To me, the role of the United States, as I
see it, in South Asia but more broadly throughout

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:59.330
East Asia, as a critical component in the
maintenance of a balance of power-that our

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:07.360
relationship with Japan and with Korea is
critical in their eyes to maintain their sense

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:10.270
of defense with regard to China.

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:13.270
And so it is with Vietnam, the Philippines.

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:16.960
And the extension: the same is true with India.

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:26.080
India sees the United States and its relationship
with us as part of its ability to secure itself

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:32.880
in the long term and manage its own relations
with a rising Chinese power.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:40.440
In addition, American political presence plays
a vital role inside of Southeast Asia and

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.660
South Asia, Pakistan and Afghanistan.

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:52.020
The Afghans look to the United States to be
a balancer in their relationship with Pakistan.

00:30:52.020 --> 00:31:00.159
The Paks have long bid for our hand in trying
to equalize matters with India.

00:31:00.159 --> 00:31:09.309
So this political structure that we have been-we
have as a nation is critical to our influence

00:31:09.309 --> 00:31:16.559
in the world and to the way the Asian nations,
including India, are prepared to look at us.

00:31:16.559 --> 00:31:23.909
So I think we have a lot more going for us
than, Stape, you would-the weight of your

00:31:23.909 --> 00:31:25.929
remarks would indicate.

00:31:25.929 --> 00:31:32.050
Moreover, and coming back to a question I
put to the panel this morning, I think we

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:34.550
have economic capability.

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:44.809
No, we will not compete with China as providing
an alternate supply chain or source of capital

00:31:44.809 --> 00:31:50.299
in the crudest sense of development banks.

00:31:50.299 --> 00:31:57.260
But TPP is our-is our brainchild, our creation.

00:31:57.260 --> 00:32:08.299
The ability to provide Asia alternatives in
Asia's trading world, economic growth, manufacturing

00:32:08.299 --> 00:32:12.840
capabilities, is very much tied to our interests.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:23.200
And I think the offer of TPP, and why nations
as diverse as Japan and Latin Americans are

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:29.330
all embracing it, precisely is to have that
economic alternative.

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:33.480
And China is today herself struggling how
she will relate to it.

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:37.340
It's a very powerful political and economic
tool.

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:44.159
So, for these two reasons, plus the military-and
I recognize its vital importance, but I literally,

00:32:44.159 --> 00:32:51.169
Stape, give it third rank in my hierarchy
of important tools available to the United

00:32:51.169 --> 00:32:52.549
States.

00:32:52.549 --> 00:32:58.140
Very important that we maintain our forward
deployments, our military-to-military ties,

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.559
our defense trade ties.

00:33:00.559 --> 00:33:04.110
All of these undergird our security system.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.630
But keep an eye on the politics and the economics.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:08.240
They're very powerful.

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:10.669
BUMILLER: Last word, quick word.

00:33:10.669 --> 00:33:16.789
FELDMAN: Yeah, I would-I would just say, I
don't-I certainly don't see the U.S. and China

00:33:16.789 --> 00:33:21.350
relationship as a-as a competitive one at
this point in South Asia, or that it shouldn't

00:33:21.350 --> 00:33:24.750
be, and one that is actually very complementary.

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:28.980
I think that we have very aligned interests
at this point in the stability in the region.

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:35.880
And it's incumbent upon us to continue to
help drive the strategic relationship and

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:41.580
some of the initiatives, and to the degree
we are engaged, we continue to be engaged

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:43.660
militarily but also economically.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:50.029
I guess I don't see-I've never seen the One
Belt, One Road as something where we would

00:33:50.029 --> 00:33:55.649
be a JV partner in because I think it fulfills
many of the ambitions that we initially laid

00:33:55.649 --> 00:34:00.490
out when we talked about the New Silk Road,
first when Secretary Clinton raised it in

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:08.839
summer 2011 in Chennai, and then the New Silk
Road summit that we had-or ministerial at

00:34:08.839 --> 00:34:10.540
UNGA that year.

00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:16.329
Yes, we're not-there was an earlier question
about Kerry-Lugar-Berman, and there were certainly

00:34:16.329 --> 00:34:22.250
significant constraints on what we were able
to achieve there, but it's quite remarkable

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:26.609
that China's commitment-whether it's 46 billion
(dollars), or at the end of the day 23 billion

00:34:26.609 --> 00:34:33.310
(dollars), or even less than that-is completely
aligned with what we prioritized for our assistance:

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:39.260
energy, infrastructure, and in fact, doing
things that we are incapable of doing abroad,

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:47.020
whether due to constraints on funding coal
projects or nuclear projects or oil and gas.

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:53.810
So we all have this common interest in a more
stable and economically sustainable Pakistan,

00:34:53.810 --> 00:35:01.320
a Pakistan that's not isolated, a South Asia
that's not, I think, riven between U.S.-aligned

00:35:01.320 --> 00:35:02.650
and China-aligned interests.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:06.700
And these are opportunities to continue to
foster and facilitate that.

00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:12.220
I hope that we can continue to develop these
tools in our own tool chest, whether on civilian

00:35:12.220 --> 00:35:17.180
assistance or doing more on trade rather than
getting stuck on-in the same discussions of

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:23.320
FTAs or BITs or anything else, and figure
out other ways to incentivize behavior and

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:24.320
contribute.

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:28.540
But this is an area that Pakistan itself has
said that they want to pivot to, to much more

00:35:28.540 --> 00:35:30.030
trade rather than aid.

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:35.000
And I guess just lastly before we open it
up for questions, I think that the regional

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:40.859
engagement here, this is a significant moment
of opportunity still, given the changes over

00:35:40.859 --> 00:35:47.060
the last few years-historic democratic transitions
of power in Afghanistan as well as Pakistan,

00:35:47.060 --> 00:35:53.599
the Modi election in India, the continued
engagement of China in the region.

00:35:53.599 --> 00:35:55.500
We'll see what happens with Iran.

00:35:55.500 --> 00:36:03.680
This should be a moment when we can capitalize
on formulating and strengthening regional

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:04.780
alliances.

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:09.380
The economic piece, I think, is the most natural
lowest-hanging fruit, and we've seen that

00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:12.880
over the last few years in CASA-1000 and TAPI.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:17.590
But there's much, much more to do, both in
terms of soft and hard linkages.

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:21.010
And I think we play a critical role in helping
to continue that trend.

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:25.460
BUMILLER: Great, thank you.

00:36:25.460 --> 00:36:32.430
OK, questions?

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:43.920
Right here in the front row, front table.

00:36:43.920 --> 00:36:44.920
Q: Hi, thanks.

00:36:44.920 --> 00:36:45.920
My name is Alan Kronstadt.

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:47.720
I'm with the Congressional Research Service.

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:52.170
And I wanted to maybe press on Pakistan a
little with Ambassador Feldman.

00:36:52.170 --> 00:36:56.970
Giving a different narrative to the story
of the last few years, you had offered maybe

00:36:56.970 --> 00:37:03.830
a more realistic engagement since 2012 had
borne fruit.

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:08.630
And one of those fruits was kind of the democratic
transition, the-you know, the election and

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:10.320
a-and a peaceful transition of power.

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:17.530
Now, there is an argument that that was less
a function of U.S. engagement and maybe advice,

00:37:17.530 --> 00:37:23.220
and more a function of Pak mil's own assessment
of its role in the society and what role it

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:26.330
wanted to play in domestic governments-governance.

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:32.450
Likewise, the military operations in the FATA
region, perhaps less a function of U.S. interest

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:38.760
and engagement and more a function of the
Pak mil's response to the Karachi airport

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:46.369
attack or the 2014 Peshawar school attack,
and that when it comes to areas that the U.S.

00:37:46.369 --> 00:37:53.230
has really been keenly interested-action against
the Haqqani Network, Lashkar-e-Taiba, Jaish-e-Mohammed;

00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:58.540
also, strengthening rather than just abiding
the existence of a democratic government-Pakistan

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:02.260
has fallen short.

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:06.079
Maybe also nuclear proliferation and planning
issues.

00:38:06.079 --> 00:38:11.570
And so, setting aside any question of the
next U.S. administration's policy toward Pakistan,

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:15.900
there have been some signs on Capitol Hill
of a-of a growing skepticism, maybe emerging

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:17.070
wariness.

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:23.920
We've seen a reduction of overall aid amounts,
coalition support funds, also non-waivable

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:28.770
conditions on coalition support funds for
the first time in the post-9/11 era, and most

00:38:28.770 --> 00:38:34.790
recently, you know, some senior members of
Congress insisting that Pakistan not be subsidized

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:38.490
in a purchase of F-16s.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:44.670
And further than that, there's analysts who
are-who are counseling for a more rigorous,

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:49.760
maybe sterner approach to Pakistan policy,
maybe even could be called punitive in some

00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:56.560
ways, as an alternative, who would reflect
upon the last four years as not in general

00:38:56.560 --> 00:39:01.740
gaining the outcomes or realizing the outcomes
that the U.S. government has sought.

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:03.573
So I'm just curious if you could respond to
that, please.

00:39:03.573 --> 00:39:04.573
FELDMAN: Sure.

00:39:04.573 --> 00:39:11.930
I mean, first of all, I mean, just to clarify,
I by no means mean to overstate what the U.S.

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:12.940
engagement led to.

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:18.410
It was not the precipitating factor for a
democratic transition.

00:39:18.410 --> 00:39:22.270
It was probably not the one for a North Waziristan
operation.

00:39:22.270 --> 00:39:29.270
Obviously, these issues are being driven by
domestic politics, by domestic security issues,

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:34.440
by domestic interests, as they would be in
virtually any country around the world.

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:40.550
I think China's engagement on counterterrorism
issues is driven far more by their concerns

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:45.220
on ETIM than by other nations seeking to engage
them in some of these things.

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:52.040
But I do think that we can look for areas
of alignment where the natural Pakistani trajectory

00:39:52.040 --> 00:39:53.590
aligns with our own interests.

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:55.660
And we have that on counterterrorism.

00:39:55.660 --> 00:39:59.580
We have that on strengthening civilian institutions.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:04.830
And by all means, I mean, I think said it
every time I testified to Congress, every

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:10.350
time I spoke publicly and privately: there
is far more that we expect of the Pakistanis

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:15.900
in addressing proxy groups, in dealing with
safe havens.

00:40:15.900 --> 00:40:25.079
This was said frequently and specifically
and strongly and repeatedly by all my predecessors,

00:40:25.079 --> 00:40:31.859
by Secretary Clinton, by Secretary Kerry,
and we have to continue to push for that.

00:40:31.859 --> 00:40:39.540
But I don't think that isolating or being
more punitive at this point is the way to

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:41.720
change a strategic calculus.

00:40:41.720 --> 00:40:45.700
And, in fact, I'm not sure that we can change
a strategic calculus.

00:40:45.700 --> 00:40:55.069
So I understand the reasons, and I don't even
necessarily disagree with more conditionality,

00:40:55.069 --> 00:40:57.650
more transparency, more reporting.

00:40:57.650 --> 00:41:03.420
But I don't think-but I think it has to be
calibrated, and we have to make sure that

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:08.540
we're looking at it over the long term in
terms of what's going to best serve our interests

00:41:08.540 --> 00:41:09.540
for the coming years.

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.940
BUMILLER: Would you like-
WISNER: Elisabeth, yeah, would you let me

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:14.420
add a short word?

00:41:14.420 --> 00:41:15.869
BUMILLER: Of course, of course.

00:41:15.869 --> 00:41:22.540
WISNER: Just over a decade ago, if my memory
serves me well-actually, slightly more than

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:30.369
a decade ago-this institution, the Council
on Foreign Relations, with the notable management

00:41:30.369 --> 00:41:36.390
of Alyssa Ayres, participation of Marshall
Bouton in the back of the room, came out with

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:42.230
a terrific study of United States relations
with South Asia.

00:41:42.230 --> 00:41:48.349
And as we looked at the question of Pakistan
from the very beginning of our relationship

00:41:48.349 --> 00:41:58.079
with Pakistan in the 1950s right through the
mid-2004/5 period, the single most dangerous

00:41:58.079 --> 00:42:05.170
element in that relationship was the roller
coaster that we put the relationship on: our

00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:14.089
willingness to engage, back off, re-engage,
impose conditions, change, back and forth,

00:42:14.089 --> 00:42:15.599
up and down.

00:42:15.599 --> 00:42:19.720
And loss of influence was just one of the
consequences.

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:26.619
And if there was one conclusion of something
that was written a decade-plus ago is, get

00:42:26.619 --> 00:42:29.940
of the roller coaster.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:35.470
Recognize that you deal with the conditions
of other nations and what you want them to

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:43.079
do over the long run by patient application
of dialogue, and you don't get it by imposing

00:42:43.079 --> 00:42:45.849
lots of short-term conditions and cutoff threats.

00:42:45.849 --> 00:42:48.030
FELDMAN: The roller-coaster analogy is very
apt.

00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:51.810
And I sometimes say, not so much that I've
experienced the arc of the relationship, but

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:54.670
the sine and cosine of the relationship over
the-over the last few years.

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:58.190
And all I'm saying is that we have to just
try to stabilize it more.

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:07.310
It has to be-it's in our own interest to continue
a much more linear and pragmatic approach

00:43:07.310 --> 00:43:11.530
rather than let ourselves be subject to these
whims, whether it's an F-16 sale with sovereign

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:14.470
funds or not, or whether-whatever the most
recent issue may be.

00:43:14.470 --> 00:43:18.290
BUMILLER: Let's see, go to the back of the
room to another member.

00:43:18.290 --> 00:43:20.630
Here in the red tie, yes.

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:21.910
Q: Hi.

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:25.119
My name is Austat (ph).

00:43:25.119 --> 00:43:26.119
I'm on the Pakistan desk-(comes on mic)-hello.

00:43:26.119 --> 00:43:31.290
I'm on the Pakistan-I'm in SRAP at the moment,
working in the-in the political component

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:32.950
of the Pakistan desk.

00:43:32.950 --> 00:43:37.050
And I have a very simple question: What will
change Pakistan's calculus?

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:41.109
FELDMAN: Shouldn't I be looking to you for
that answer?

00:43:41.109 --> 00:43:42.109
(Laughter, laughs.)

00:43:42.109 --> 00:43:44.339
Q: I'm searching for wisdom.

00:43:44.339 --> 00:43:45.339
(Laughter.)

00:43:45.339 --> 00:43:46.339
FELDMAN: (Laughs.)

00:43:46.339 --> 00:43:50.810
I think we have to-I mean, there's plenty
of Pakistan experts here, including the woman

00:43:50.810 --> 00:43:54.990
to your right and many others.

00:43:54.990 --> 00:44:01.730
I think that-I think, again, in the interest
of being realistic and clear-eyed, we have

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:03.990
to recognize what we can and cannot do.

00:44:03.990 --> 00:44:08.950
And, you know, there was a lot of talk earlier
in the administration about what will change

00:44:08.950 --> 00:44:10.410
the strategic calculus.

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:13.490
And I think that, at the end of the day, it's
very, very little.

00:44:13.490 --> 00:44:20.430
It's working with them to-if there are issues,
as perhaps the Peshawar incident was, if there

00:44:20.430 --> 00:44:27.260
are others that can demonstrate that they
have a vested interest in dealing with these

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:33.160
issues in their own society for the stability
of their own country, to do what is right

00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:39.040
by the Pakistani entrepreneurs, to try to
grow a civil society, that they have to take

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:43.940
certain steps-which we can help them on, but
we can't do for them-that that is ultimately

00:44:43.940 --> 00:44:48.349
the realization that we all have to help encourage.

00:44:48.349 --> 00:44:52.109
BUMILLER: Yes, here on the left in the salmon-colored
sweater.

00:44:52.109 --> 00:44:54.220
Q: Thanks very much.

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:55.790
I'm Garrett Mitchell.

00:44:55.790 --> 00:44:57.890
I write The Mitchell Report.

00:44:57.890 --> 00:45:07.230
And on the day when Donald Trump has become
I think officially the presumptive nominee

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:13.119
of the Republican Party, I want to pose a
question that I-if it sounds facetious, it

00:45:13.119 --> 00:45:14.990
is not intended to be.

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:21.830
But I was thinking about having a conversation
like this in a room that isn't filled with

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:27.570
people who associate with the Council on Foreign
Relations, but filled with people who are

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:38.470
leaning in the Trump direction, however one
wants to characterize them-isolationist, nativist,

00:45:38.470 --> 00:45:40.980
or something less pejorative.

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:47.710
I was wondering how we might frame some of
the thoughts that you've been expressing,

00:45:47.710 --> 00:45:55.910
but without vocabulary that isn't going to
fly with apparently a lot of voters.

00:45:55.910 --> 00:46:00.619
They don't know about strategic alliances
or strategic partnerships.

00:46:00.619 --> 00:46:05.880
They're not-they don't know what a TTIP is,
they know what a Q-tip is.

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:12.620
How do we-how do we-I'm not suggesting that
we-that we all learn how to talk like Donald

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:20.210
Trump, but how do we-how do we make these
points to an audience of people who need to

00:46:20.210 --> 00:46:24.940
hear something different, but who also need
to understand the validity of what you're

00:46:24.940 --> 00:46:25.940
talking about?

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:28.099
BUMILLER: That's a very good question.

00:46:28.099 --> 00:46:30.770
Who wants to take a bite at that?

00:46:30.770 --> 00:46:33.270
WISNER: Stape?

00:46:33.270 --> 00:46:34.520
(Laughter.)

00:46:34.520 --> 00:46:38.260
I'm happy to.

00:46:38.260 --> 00:46:47.230
ROY: As I understand Donald Trump's position,
it's that our allies are having a free ride,

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:54.400
and that they should either pay the freight
or we'll get off the bus.

00:46:54.400 --> 00:46:59.060
I think if he were to be elected president,
he would discover that you can't deal with

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:05.840
the world outside of our borders on that basis.

00:47:05.840 --> 00:47:11.130
In other words, diplomacy is the business
of influencing people, and offering people

00:47:11.130 --> 00:47:20.910
take-it-or-leave-it propositions generally
is not a good generic way of influencing them.

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:26.920
But I'm going to draw on some earlier comments
here, because I have generally-I generally

00:47:26.920 --> 00:47:34.570
agree with what Frank Wisner and Dan said,
but I'd package it differently.

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:39.950
I think the problem that whoever becomes the
U.S. president is going to discover is that

00:47:39.950 --> 00:47:45.410
having the largest economy in the world is
irrelevant in public policy terms.

00:47:45.410 --> 00:47:50.579
In public policy terms, what is the criterion
of the strength of your economy is the ability

00:47:50.579 --> 00:47:55.849
of the political system to extract the economic
resources necessary to play a great power

00:47:55.849 --> 00:47:57.069
role.

00:47:57.069 --> 00:48:02.740
We went into World War II totally unprepared,
not because our economy was so weak it couldn't

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:07.960
be prepared, but because our political system
would not permit us to become prepared.

00:48:07.960 --> 00:48:12.910
At the moment, our political system is funding
the military component of our international

00:48:12.910 --> 00:48:20.190
presence and is grossly underfunding all of
the other aspects of our comprehensive power

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:26.480
in a world where we are cooperating with and
competing with rising powers such as China

00:48:26.480 --> 00:48:27.480
and India.

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:32.310
I also think that we have to make some important
distinctions in looking at the world.

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:39.540
In East Asia, our balancing role is vital,
and India is a bit player in this respect.

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:44.030
In South Asia, I would offer you it's not
the United States who can play a balancing

00:48:44.030 --> 00:48:46.920
role, because India wants to play the balancing
role.

00:48:46.920 --> 00:48:51.819
And what we have discovered in our relationship
with India is it's so determined to be the

00:48:51.819 --> 00:48:55.150
balancer that it won't get into the U.S. pocket.

00:48:55.150 --> 00:48:57.440
That's why it doesn't buy U.S. aircraft.

00:48:57.440 --> 00:49:01.740
It buys them from other people, even though
we thought that our new relationship was going

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:04.380
to guarantee us some military sales.

00:49:04.380 --> 00:49:06.950
It doesn't work out that way.

00:49:06.950 --> 00:49:11.140
Pakistan does not consider us a reliable ally
because of this roller coaster that we have

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:13.190
had repeatedly there.

00:49:13.190 --> 00:49:19.170
China is the factor that's important to Pakistan
because it is reliable, it's there, it gives

00:49:19.170 --> 00:49:21.980
it military support-up to limits.

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:27.440
Let's not forget that when we've had Pakistan-India
rows, China doesn't throw its national interest

00:49:27.440 --> 00:49:31.220
in vainly to try to alter that balance.

00:49:31.220 --> 00:49:35.820
But the fact of the matter is that China is
seen by Pakistan as a reliable ally and the

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:40.280
United States is seen as one-as a fair-weather
friend.

00:49:40.280 --> 00:49:45.170
Simply the history of the last 10 years-why
did we develop the Northern Transportation

00:49:45.170 --> 00:49:46.170
Network?

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:51.990
It was because Pakistan wouldn't permit us
to move goods through Pakistan to Afghanistan.

00:49:51.990 --> 00:49:56.170
BUMILLER: But what do you say a to a roomful
of Trump supporters right now to explain what

00:49:56.170 --> 00:49:57.170
you just said?

00:49:57.170 --> 00:50:02.430
What is the simple explanation for why the
United States has to engage in the world?

00:50:02.430 --> 00:50:05.760
What do you tell-I'm not going to say it,
actually-but what do you-(laughter)-

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:07.440
WISNER: Got it.

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:08.560
ROY: OK.

00:50:08.560 --> 00:50:09.849
FELDMAN: What's the bumper sticker?

00:50:09.849 --> 00:50:11.650
WISNER: Well, let me-
ROY: Very briefly-

00:50:11.650 --> 00:50:13.410
BUMILLER: OK, anybody.

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:14.410
And yeah-
WISNER: I'll follow.

00:50:14.410 --> 00:50:18.710
ROY: Since the beginning of our Republic,
the economic factor has been the basis of

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:20.619
our national strength.

00:50:20.619 --> 00:50:27.140
At the moment, China has a stronger trade
relationship with all of our friends and allies

00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:28.950
in East Asia.

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:34.590
China has more trade-is the largest trading
partner of India, more so than we are.

00:50:34.590 --> 00:50:40.900
In other words, in dealing with the world,
the United States is going to be a loser if

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:47.050
we are not able to play a role other than
the military factor, which is our trump card

00:50:47.050 --> 00:50:50.420
at the moment, if you will, is that military
factor.

00:50:50.420 --> 00:50:54.250
BUMILLER: But we're losing jobs here because
of that.

00:50:54.250 --> 00:50:55.260
What do you say to that?

00:50:55.260 --> 00:50:58.700
WISNER: Well, it's-I think-
ROY: I say we lose jobs when job move from

00:50:58.700 --> 00:51:01.740
Massachusetts to North Carolina.

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:06.760
The fact that we're moving it over-the reason
why East Asia has been a success story is

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:12.150
because the jobs move where they are economically
most properly placed.

00:51:12.150 --> 00:51:14.330
It's not the question of losing jobs.

00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:19.960
It's that our jobs-the jobs that we have lost
are not jobs that belong in the United States,

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:24.140
but we haven't substituted the jobs that belong
in the United States where we have the competitive

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:26.040
advantage.

00:51:26.040 --> 00:51:30.650
And that's the transformation that we have
to make in our own economy and we need to

00:51:30.650 --> 00:51:33.530
have the government policies that will facilitate
that.

00:51:33.530 --> 00:51:37.050
BUMILLER: Did you have a-
WISNER: I like that, yeah.

00:51:37.050 --> 00:51:41.390
I would particularly state the last thing
you said.

00:51:41.390 --> 00:51:45.609
And it goes, I believe to the question you
put to us.

00:51:45.609 --> 00:51:51.109
I think whoever wins this election-Donald
Trump or Hillary Clinton, or whoever wins

00:51:51.109 --> 00:51:53.099
it-(laughter)-we're going to be faced with
the-

00:51:53.099 --> 00:51:54.099
BUMILLER: Paul Ryan.

00:51:54.099 --> 00:51:56.910
WISNER: We've had enough surprises this far.

00:51:56.910 --> 00:51:57.910
(Laughter.)

00:51:57.910 --> 00:52:04.710
Whoever wins it, we're faced with an American
public that is deeply skeptical of use of

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:10.990
expressions like national interest and trade
agreements.

00:52:10.990 --> 00:52:17.410
And we have a job of rebuilding a consensus
behind policy again.

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:23.970
We have a country that has not recovered fully
from our recession and a country that has

00:52:23.970 --> 00:52:30.230
overextended-believes its overextended itself
in wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:52:30.230 --> 00:52:36.030
So how do address the question of-I think
you're absolutely right.

00:52:36.030 --> 00:52:41.900
Set aside words like national interest and
go to the heart of the matter.

00:52:41.900 --> 00:52:48.580
What's going to produce American power, so
that we have influence in the world where

00:52:48.580 --> 00:52:54.380
we and our wishes in the world will be listened
to by other nations?

00:52:54.380 --> 00:52:59.730
And that's a mixture of the strength of our
military, the skill of our diplomacy, the

00:52:59.730 --> 00:53:01.890
quality of our intelligence.

00:53:01.890 --> 00:53:09.390
And it's there through our alliance system,
and being able to multiply our message through

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:17.230
the mouths of close associates and allies
around the world, and be feared when necessary,

00:53:17.230 --> 00:53:20.410
but have the power to deliver, point one.

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:23.690
Point two is economics.

00:53:23.690 --> 00:53:27.089
It's all good and well to say trade makes
jobs.

00:53:27.089 --> 00:53:37.280
But unless we can prove to Americans that
when we displace that job in Pottstown, Pennsylvania

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:44.690
and move it to China or to Malaysia, and replace
it with a higher-paying job and help the worker

00:53:44.690 --> 00:53:52.069
who is losing in Pennsylvania find his way
forward, we've really-we've really, really

00:53:52.069 --> 00:53:56.319
not addressed a major societal problem.

00:53:56.319 --> 00:54:02.329
And I think when I look at the prospect of
selling TPP, for example, leave the word out

00:54:02.329 --> 00:54:09.501
of it, trade agreements of any sort, I think
the two great selling points we're going to

00:54:09.501 --> 00:54:13.400
have to focus on is, number one, trade adjustment.

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:20.619
How do you create inside the structure of
your agreements the ability to train your

00:54:20.619 --> 00:54:26.349
people and upgrade their skills, and make
that real, and help people move from point

00:54:26.349 --> 00:54:33.270
A in the economy to point B. Second, I think
it's going to be very important to deal with

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:35.630
the environmental question.

00:54:35.630 --> 00:54:43.341
And that is, how we use trade agreements to
improve the life we live, the quality of the

00:54:43.341 --> 00:54:49.339
air we breathe, the environment and the climate
that we are a part of.

00:54:49.339 --> 00:54:56.261
If I were to sit down and re-explain TPP today,
and look at a Trump voter in the face, I would

00:54:56.261 --> 00:55:01.960
be able to say: I'll deliver better on those
two questions than anyone who talks simply

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:03.299
about tearing up an agreement.

00:55:03.299 --> 00:55:05.799
BUMILLER: Dan-
FELDMAN: Yeah, I'll just say very, very quickly.

00:55:05.799 --> 00:55:08.970
I think it's a great question and I concur
with everything that's been said.

00:55:08.970 --> 00:55:13.569
And I think that it's something that the campaign-the
Clinton campaign will be struggling with for

00:55:13.569 --> 00:55:15.829
the next six months.

00:55:15.829 --> 00:55:20.270
Putting aside the question that I'm not actually
sure what any of Donald Trump's policies would

00:55:20.270 --> 00:55:28.490
be on this, but I would say that boiling it
down to a bare minimum that the U.S. cannot

00:55:28.490 --> 00:55:30.869
be great if we do it in isolation.

00:55:30.869 --> 00:55:34.329
That at the end of the day, it's not just
about national security interests, but better

00:55:34.329 --> 00:55:37.950
defining and communicating what that means
to these voters.

00:55:37.950 --> 00:55:43.839
And I would say we cannot be as safe as possible
and we cannot be as prosperous as possible

00:55:43.839 --> 00:55:47.340
without our engagement with the rest of the
global community.

00:55:47.340 --> 00:55:50.540
And certainly-and then demonstrate examples
by which that's true.

00:55:50.540 --> 00:55:55.599
I think in my case, in Afghanistan, it could
not be more evident that we would not have

00:55:55.599 --> 00:56:00.900
been able to accomplish what we've accomplished
thus far, and recognizing that it's still

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:07.549
extremely fragile, but without NATO, without
the international community.

00:56:07.549 --> 00:56:12.090
And as we set up our-the contact group that
Marc talked about with the other special representatives

00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:18.150
from 50 other countries engaged, we made sure
that it was-there was about a third from Muslim-majority

00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:22.660
countries, so it wasn't just a clash of civilizations,
that the regional component continues to be

00:56:22.660 --> 00:56:23.660
important.

00:56:23.660 --> 00:56:25.109
And that can't be driven by us.

00:56:25.109 --> 00:56:30.400
We can help to facilitate and nurture it,
but we have to continue to encourage that.

00:56:30.400 --> 00:56:34.800
And we don't have a voice at that table, which
is in our own interest if we're not engaged.

00:56:34.800 --> 00:56:42.359
But I do think it has to be boiled down to
that minimal-to that degree of a talking point.

00:56:42.359 --> 00:56:45.049
BUMILLER: Well, thank you to all three of
you.

00:56:45.049 --> 00:56:46.330
Thank you to the members.

00:56:46.330 --> 00:56:47.940
And that concludes the panel.

00:56:47.940 --> 00:56:48.940
(Applause.)

00:56:48.940 --> 00:56:48.941
(END)

